   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\">Hi all,</span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\"><br></span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\">I have been analysing some camera trapping data using secr for the first time, with a mark-resight model, and am unsure about my choice of buffer. I understand that the density should reach an asymptote with increasing buffer width, and by running the model multiple times with different buffer widths, I can see this happening. However, the density only levels out at around 8km, which seems way too large for what we know about the home ranges/movements of these animals. I&#39;d expect a sensible buffer to be about 4km. </span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\"><br aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\">I tried comparing the hazard function vs. half-normal but these gave similar results. I am not sure what to try next, or whether to leave the buffer at 4km or 8km. Any ideas would be welcome. I&#39;m new to this type of analysis so this might be a simple issue!</span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\"><br></span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\">Many thanks,</span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\">Erica</span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"border:0px;font-variant:inherit;font-weight:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:11pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline\"><br></span></p><p style=\"color:rgb(36,36,36);font-size:15px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px\"><br></p>"
    ]
